PUBLIC RESPONSE: A. Clockwise direction, origin near ton

On the conventional 100 min E. coli circular chromosome azi/azis lies in the leu-azi-his region ~ 40–44 min.  tonB is ~ 27 min, thr is ~ 27 min, pro is ~ 52–55 min, lac is ~ 8 min and strA is ~ 80–85 min on the same clockwise increasing-minutes map.

In an Hfr a marker is first expressed the sooner the closer it lies to the Hfr origin on the direction in which the chromosome is pumped out.  The earliest arrival of azi is obtained with an origin in the mid-20s min and transfer in the increasing-minutes, i.e. clockwise, sense toward the 40 min region.

* origin ≈ ton ≈ 27 min clockwise → ton → … → azi ≈ 27 → 43 min ≈ 15–16 min of transfer time. azi therefore enters the recipient early and is present / expressed well before the bulk of the chromosome that follows it has arrived, giving the observed prolonged early expression of azis.

The other choices place azi much further downstream on the transfer order:

* B. lac ≈ 8 min counterclockwise is ≈ 65 min of transfer away from azi;
* C. pro ≈ 52 min clockwise reaches azi only after wrapping round the chromosome ∼ 85–90 min downstream;
* D. thr ≈ 27 min counterclockwise goes the long way round to azi ≈ 85 min downstream;
* E. str ≈ 80–85 min clockwise reaches azi only after ≈ 60–65 min of transfer.

Hence the ton-proximal clockwise Hfr gives the early, prolonged azi expression that is seen.

